# ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
# │ LESSON 3 · Waveforms and harmonics                   │
# └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
#
# Play this and listen: the same note (220 Hz) in four
# different "colours", three seconds each, round and round:
#
#   sine → triangle → square → sawtooth
#
# The pitch never changes, yet each one sounds different.
# That difference is called TIMBRE, and it comes from
# HARMONICS: extra sine waves riding on top of the note at
# 2×, 3×, 4×... its frequency.
#
#   sine      just the note (220). Pure, flute-like.
#   triangle  odd harmonics only (660, 1100...), very quiet.
#             Soft, a bit hollow.
#   square    odd harmonics, louder (each 1/n). Hollow,
#             clarinet-like, retro video game.
#   sawtooth  ALL harmonics (440, 660, 880...), each 1/n.
#             Buzzy and bright, like a bowed string or brass.
#
# The rule to remember: a waveform's shape IS its recipe of
# harmonics. Sharp corners mean lots of high harmonics, so a
# bright sound. Smooth curves mean few, so a mellow sound.
#
# ▶ NEW IDEAS IN THE CODE
#
#   t          seconds since the engine started
#   $elapsed   state: it keeps its value from one sample to
#              the next. Adding dt (one sample's time) each
#              sample makes it the seconds since this piece began.
#   let pos    a name for a value. pos = seconds since send.
#   int(x)     drops the fraction: int(4.7) = 4
#   mod        remainder: 5 mod 4 = 1. So "which" counts
#              0, 1, 2, 3, 0, 1... every 3 seconds.
#   if/else    picks one value. Each else-if is a new line.

play tour:
  $elapsed   = 0
  $elapsed   = $elapsed + dt
  let pos   = $elapsed
  let which = int(pos / 3) mod 4
  if which == 0 then osc(sin, 220) * 0.2
  else if which == 1 then osc(tri, 220) * 0.2
  else if which == 2 then osc(sqr, 220) * 0.08
  else osc(saw, 220) * 0.12

tour

# ▶ TRY THIS
#
#   1. See the harmonics. Make a copy that plays only the saw
#      (replace the whole if/else with osc(saw, 220) * 0.12),
#      then:   ./aither audit edc/03_waveforms.aither 2
#      You'll see peaks at 220, 440, 660, 880... getting
#      quieter. Try sqr: 440 and 880 disappear.
#   2. Why are square and saw multiplied by less? They pack
#      more energy into the same height. Try 0.2 on all four
#      and hear the square jump out.
#   3. Change 3 in pos / 3 to 0.5 for a fast colour strobe.
#   4. Make your own waveform: any function works as a shape.
#        def wobbly(x): sin(x) + sin(3 * x) * 0.3
#      Put that line at the top of the file and use
#      osc(wobbly, 220). That's a sine plus its 3rd harmonic.
#
# ▶ WORTH KNOWING
#
#   osc(saw, ...) and osc(sqr, ...) are "naive" waveforms:
#   their harmonics never stop, so the ones above what the
#   computer can represent fold back as faint wrong notes
#   ("aliasing"). Great for chiptune grit. Lesson 5 shows
#   the clean way: build the harmonics yourself.
#
# ▶ PUZZLE → edc/03_waveforms_puzzle.aither
